<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <description>Read on the loan fields the portal renders and create, through LosCreateApplication, on the fields an application carries. Risk_Rating__c, LTV__c, DSCR__c and Underwriting_Notes__c stay absent: the bank&apos;s assessment. Rows come from the sharing set.</description>
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>LOS Borrower Portal</label>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosBoxTokenService</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <!--
      Provisioning is the borrower's first request after creating an application: the
      workspace asks the package for the loan's folder and, when there is none yet, asks
      this class to create one. Without the grant the new loan opens on a 404 it can never
      leave. The class binds the folder to the record and grants nothing else.
    -->
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosBoxFolderService</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosBorrowerLoans</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosWhoAmI</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosCreateApplication</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>LosClassifyDocument</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Loan_ID__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Borrower_Account__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>false</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Maturity_Date__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Status__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Term_Months__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>false</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Box_Workspace_Folder_ID__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Loan_Type__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Borrower_Entity__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Borrower__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Loan_Amount__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <!--
      Editable, not just readable, on the fields an application carries. LosCreateApplication
      inserts in user mode, so the borrower&apos;s own field-level security is what decides
      which fields a new loan may be born with; a field missing here fails the insert. Object
      edit stays off below, so editable here means "may be set on a record this user creates",
      not "may be changed afterwards". Rate, LTV, DSCR, collateral value, risk and officer
      are not listed, and so cannot be set from the portal by any route.
    -->
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Record_Source__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Applicant_Name__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Applicant_Email__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Purpose__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>true</editable>
        <field>LOS_Loan__c.Collateral_Type__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <!--
      The Box for Salesforce record-to-folder association, read only. LosClassifyDocument
      bounds a file to its loan through LosLoanPackage, which reads box__FRUP__c with SOQL;
      a user who cannot see the object gets "sObject type box__FRUP__c is not supported"
      rather than an empty result, and a loan the portal just created has no folder id on
      the record itself to fall back to. The token endpoint sidesteps this by asking the
      package, which is why it never needed the grant. A folder id grants nothing: reaching
      the folder still takes a token minted per record, and only for a record this user can
      read. LOS_Loan_Agent carries the same grant for the same reason.
    -->
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>false</editable>
        <field>box__FRUP__c.box__Folder_ID__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <fieldPermissions>
        <editable>false</editable>
        <field>box__FRUP__c.box__Record_ID__c</field>
        <readable>true</readable>
    </fieldPermissions>
    <!--
      Apex REST is what mints the Box token, and a call to /services/apexrest is refused
      with a bare 403 unless the caller has API Enabled. The Customer Community profile
      does not grant it. That is why the workspace could list loans and then fail to
      load a single document: the GraphQL UI API goes through the site's own bridge and
      needs no such permission, so one path worked while the other did not.

      It widens nothing this user can reach. API Enabled governs whether the platform will
      answer them at all, not what it answers with; every call is still bounded by the
      object read and create, the fields listed here, and the sharing set below.
    -->
    <userPermissions>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <name>ApiEnabled</name>
    </userPermissions>
    <objectPermissions>
        <allowCreate>true</allowCreate>
        <allowDelete>false</allowDelete>
        <allowEdit>false</allowEdit>
        <allowRead>true</allowRead>
        <modifyAllRecords>false</modifyAllRecords>
        <object>LOS_Loan__c</object>
        <!--
          Read and create, never edit or delete. No records are shared with a borrower, so
          read grants the ability to run the query, not the ability to see anybody's rows;
          the sharing set on Borrower_Account__c decides which rows come back. Create is
          what LosCreateApplication needs to insert in user mode; the record it creates is
          bound to the borrower's own account by the class, and once created the borrower
          cannot change it. Edit stays off so the bank's assessment is never theirs to alter.
        -->
        <viewAllRecords>false</viewAllRecords>
    </objectPermissions>
    <objectPermissions>
        <allowCreate>false</allowCreate>
        <allowDelete>false</allowDelete>
        <allowEdit>false</allowEdit>
        <allowRead>true</allowRead>
        <modifyAllRecords>false</modifyAllRecords>
        <object>box__FRUP__c</object>
        <viewAllRecords>false</viewAllRecords>
    </objectPermissions>
    <!--
      Minting the Box token is a callout under the org's Client Credentials Grant, and Apex
      cannot use that credential on a user's behalf unless the user is granted the
      principal. Without both of these the endpoint is reached, runs, and throws
      System.CalloutException, which surfaces to the browser as box_request_failed with no
      indication that a permission is missing. LOS_Box_Preview_Guest and LOS_Loan_Agent
      both carry the same pair for the same reason.
    -->
    <objectPermissions>
        <allowCreate>false</allowCreate>
        <allowDelete>false</allowDelete>
        <allowEdit>false</allowEdit>
        <allowRead>true</allowRead>
        <modifyAllRecords>false</modifyAllRecords>
        <object>UserExternalCredential</object>
        <viewAllRecords>false</viewAllRecords>
    </objectPermissions>
    <externalCredentialPrincipalAccesses>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <externalCredentialPrincipal>LOS_Box-LOS_Box_Principal</externalCredentialPrincipal>
    </externalCredentialPrincipalAccesses>
</PermissionSet>
